0 24px/31px nyt-cheltenham,georgia,serif;margin:0 0 10px"><span>A degree=3F=
 It=E2=80=99s not what it used to be.</span></h2><p style=3D"display:block;=
color:#333;font:400 18.5px/26px georgia,serif;margin:0 0 10px"><span>By Ama=
nda Taub</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#3=
33;margin:0 0 15px"><span>In many ways, France and India could hardly be mo=
re different. But the monumental protests that have shaken both countries t=
his fall have one striking similarity. They have been led by students who h=
ave revolted in response to the failed promises of education.</span></p><p =
style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><s=
pan>In India, students took to the streets after the government abruptly ca=
nceled the results of a medical-school admissions exam because test questio=
ns had been leaked online, forcing everyone to take the test again. Young p=
eople across the country saw the scandal as emblematic of a broken educatio=
n system.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#=
333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>In France, students in poor suburbs outside Pari=
s began protesting the decrepit physical condition of their schools, an opa=
que university admissions system and absent teachers. Soon, the demonstrati=
ons spread across the country and, in some cities, spiraled into rioting.</=
span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:=
0 0 15px"><span>Different as the countries may be, the unrest has a shared =
source of outrage: the breakdown of a foundational social contract that one=
 could call the meritocratic bargain.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17p=
x/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>It=E2=80=99s a promi=
se at the core of many countries=E2=80=99 political compacts. Though specif=
ics vary, the central proposition is that a fair society rewards merit, and=
 that educational achievements are a good measure of merit because educatio=
n is open to all and leads to the acquisition of valuable skills. Therefore=
, education should be a route to social and economic advancement.</span></p=
><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px=
"><span>The value of this system is not just that it selects for those with=
 high ability, but also that it offers a justification for the resulting so=
cioeconomic hierarchy =E2=80=94 including who ends up at the bottom.</span>=
</p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 1=
5px"><span>In many places around the world, that system has proved to be re=
markably effective, in large part because education </span><span style=3D"f=
ont-style:italic">has</span><span> translated relatively straightforwardly =
into economic mobility. But increasingly, many who work hard at higher educ=
ation are finding that the rewards are no longer there at the end.</span></=
p><table border=3D"0" cellpadding=3D"0" cellspacing=3D"0" style=3D"width:10=
0%"><tbody><tr><td style=3D"text-align:center"><img width=3D"500" height=3D=
"624" style=3D"display:inline-block;width:83%;max-width:500px;height:auto;p=
adding-top:10px;padding-bottom:8px" class=3D"css-18ei5jy" src=3D"https://st=
atic01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/26/multimedia/26int-theworld-students-01-hbqt=
/26int-theworld-students-01-hbqt-jumbo.jpg" alt=3D"A large group of people =
chant outdoors while holding handwritten signs."></td></tr><tr><td style=3D=
"text-align:center;width:100%;padding-bottom:15px;line-height:14px" class=
=3D"css-1b9cqt1"><span style=3D"display:inline-block;width:83%;max-width:50=
0px;text-align:left" class=3D"css-14cus5q"><span style=3D"color:#666;font:n=
ormal 14px georgia,serif">Student protests in New Delhi, India, in July.=C2=
=A0</span><span style=3D"margin:0;font:11px/11px georgia,serif;color:#888;l=
etter-spacing:.01em">Saumya Khandelwal for The New York Times</span></span>=
</td></tr></tbody></table><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;c=
olor:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">A deal with cost=
s</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;marg=
in:0 0 15px"><span>The resulting discontent is not limited to France and In=
dia.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;m=
argin:0 0 15px"><span>Young Americans voted in droves in the recent midterm=
 elections for Democratic candidates who tapped into their frustration over=
 being saddled with college debt and stuck in low-paying jobs. Serbian stud=
ent protests grew into a political movement that led to the ouster of Aleks=
andar Vucic, the country=E2=80=99s longtime nationalist strongman, in an el=
ection last month. Last week saw yet more protests by young people across E=
urope.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333=
;margin:0 0 15px"><span>It=E2=80=99s all a reminder that the meritocratic b=
argain as we understand it today, in which university education is a steppi=
ngstone to middle-class careers, can=E2=80=99t be taken for granted.</span>=
</p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 1=
5px"><span>England didn=E2=80=99t introduce free, universal secondary educa=
tion until 1944. In the U.S., it took the G.I. Bill during World War II to =
open broader access to college. And India introduced the first national col=
lege entrance exam only in the 1960s.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17p=
x/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>For individuals, thi=
s access to education promised more money, more status or both. For governm=
ents, it was a way to convince people to buy into society as currently stru=
ctured, by promising the potential for advancement.</span></p><p style=3D"f=
ont:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But th=
at proposition comes at a cost. In India, South Korea, the U.S. and elsewhe=
re, education-based meritocracy places significant burdens on young people =
and their families, whether through years of grueling study or tens of thou=
sands of dollars in debt.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georg=
ia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>That sacrifice helps explain why=
, just as the meritocratic bargain can be a source of social cohesion, its =
breakdown can also become a trigger for social explosion.</span></p><p styl=
e=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>=
It has happened before. In 1905, for instance, China abolished the national=
 civil service exam that had for 1,300 years been a way to recruit elites. =
The shattered expectations of frustrated, aspiring commoners drove them to =
join revolutionary uprisings that eventually overthrew the imperial dynasty=
 in 1911. Disillusioned scholars also flocked to revolutionary organization=
s in search of alternative routes to advancement.</span></p><table border=
=3D"0" cellpadding=3D"0" cellspacing=3D"0" style=3D"width:100%"><tbody><tr>=
